WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.440
Arguably the most natural and powerful form of learning

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.100
is through experience,

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.440
or more precisely through reflection on doing.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.260
Also called experiential learning,

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.280
it’s what prima ballerinas do

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.900
after their performance at the national opera.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:19.740
But it also happens to boys that are sad

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:21.260
because their father got angry

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:24.060
when they played football in the living room.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.580
By the age of one,

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.660
we all had our own painful encounter with

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.180
experiential learning

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.520
when we tried to walk, failed,

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.580
fell and cried like, well a baby…

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.760
And even though this was an unpleasant and

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.060
discouraging exercise that lasted for months

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.040
in the end

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.460
we all made it.

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:46.420
How is that possible?

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.240
As soon as we fell and the first shock was over,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.700
our brain unconsciously began to make sense

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.900
out of all of the information available

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.720
to identify how this embarrassment occurred.

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.540
It remembers that when we pushed ourselves up,

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.180
everything was fine:

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:04.720
our feet on the floor,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.080
our arms in position and our head

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.620
and shoulders up right.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:10.320
Ready to go!

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:13.320
When our upper leg muscles

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.600
pulled our left foot 12.3% to the front

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:18.780
at an angle of 23 degree,

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.160
our arms didn’t compliment the movement

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.340
and the ventricles in the inner ear,

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.460
responsible for static balance,

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:27.180
got confused for a second.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:30.300
When at the same moment the cat ran by,

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.500
our eyes sent an alarming signal to the hippocampus

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.200
and we completely lost it…

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:36.780
Outch!

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.020
Unconsciously this is how our brain analyses

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:43.720
the relationship of events within our body

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.480
or in the environment.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.520
It happens all the time as we learn to walk,

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:49.300
talk,

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:50.060
kiss,

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.360
function in a fancy office

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.340
or dance the salsa.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.100
Once we understand the connections between

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.260
what went wrong,

00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.800
we know what we need to change

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:01.540
when we try the next time.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:04.500
Experiential Learning

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.700
can also be used explicitly to learn a new skill

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.860
or to become better at what we already love doing.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.180
Here is how it works:

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:17.180
First get yourself into a situation to experience.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.020
After, reflect on what happened.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.820
Then try to understand the relationships

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.000
to form an abstract concept

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.260
- if I do A,

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.520
I get B.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.440
Last, decide what to do differently next time.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.260
Then do it again.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:35.800
For example

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.860
if you want to learn how to ride a bicycle,

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.760
the following might happen:

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.540
You get onto the bike and start kicking the pedals.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.440
As you are afraid of speed,

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.460
you ride too slowly and you fall to the side.

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:49.940
Afterward

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.940
you remember that you fell at the exact moment

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.160
when your speed was the slowest

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:58.140
Then you form the abstract and slightly dangerous idea:

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:00.700
speed equals stability

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.440
Next time, you decide, you’ll speed up

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.360
Lets try that again:

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:09.580
This time you ride fast.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.900
In fact, so fast that you crash straight into the next fruit stall.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:15.840
Your head hurts

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.440
and you realize that speeding like that is really dangerous.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.920
You create the next abstract thought:

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:25.860
speed is danger and danger needs protection

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.920
You then decide to wear a helmet next time

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.620
Experiential learning is also believed to be responsible

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:34.980
for the fact that musicians

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:37.460
generally fare better at most tests,

00:03:37.460 --> 00:03:39.500
regardless of what they measure.

00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:41.760
People that practice an instrument

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.200
not only engage their brain in motor,

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.200
visual and auditory areas,

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.160
but they also learn by reflecting on what they’re doing

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.960
with a fast feedback loop

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.240
– a wrong tone on the violin sounds too terrible

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.900
to remain unnoticed.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.540
While playing they therefore not only learn to make music,

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.980
but also that progress in general comes through practice,

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:04.940
reflection,

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.040
understanding,

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:07.560
and repetition.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:10.300
You can use it with your friends

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.620
or colleagues when working on a project.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:14.640
Silicon Valley start-ups do it

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.760
when they tell their developers to get out of the building!

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.140
After the interaction with real potential customers,

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.660
the team gets together,

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:24.300
analyses the feedback

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:26.380
and decides what to do next.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.080
If you are all by yourself learning something

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.120
where feedback doesn’t come fast and painfully,

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:34.100
get going

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:34.860
and look for someone

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:37.320
who can give you a second opinion along the way

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.860
for students that can be a friend or the teacher,

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.200
for entrepreneurs that’s the mentor

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.940
and for journalists a blog where readers can comment.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:50.340
Tell us, what do you think?

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.740
Is learning through reflecting on doing

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.840
only good when acquiring new hands-on skills

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:58.720
or is it also suitable to study science,

00:04:58.720 --> 00:04:59.520
math,

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:00.620
the humanities

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:02.520
or abstract art?

